1
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:07,040
Now that we have discussed deadlocks, 
let's look at a classic problem that,  

2
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:13,840
if not properly handled, can lead to deadlocks.
I'm calling this the chopstick-variation of the  

3
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:18,400
dining philosophers' problem; the original 
problem has been introduced by Dijkstra. 

4
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:25,600
Suppose we have n philosophers (they 
represent our threads) and n chopsticks. 

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:32,160
Each philosopher would like to eat, but to do 
so he/she needs 2 chopsticks, the one on their  

6
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:37,600
left side and the one on their right side.
Once a philosopher gets both chopsticks,  

7
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:42,080
others of course cannot use the same 
chopsticks used by the philosopher. 

8
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:48,720
If you reflect about it, you will see that if we 
have 2 philosophers only one can eat at a time,  

9
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:54,320
if we have 3 philosophers only 1 can 
eat at a time, if we have 4 philosophers  

10
00:00:54,320 --> 00:01:00,000
than 2 can eat in parallel (but they 
cannot be adjacent ones), and so on. 

11
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:06,400
So, can this setup lead to deadlock 
if our algorithm is poorly designed?

12
00:01:07,760 --> 00:01:10,960
Let's have a look and let us 
consider the following solution,  

13
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:12,960
which we call pick-left-and-then-pick-right.

14
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:19,600
The first thing we have to do is to 
check if the mutual exclusion property is  

15
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:24,880
satisfied or not. I leave this 
to you as a homework (again,  

16
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:28,160
reason formally about whether the 
property is satisfied or not).

17
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:33,680
The second thing we want to check 
is whether deadlocks can occur. 

18
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:39,280
From our previous discussion, if 
mutual exclusion, hold and wait,  

19
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:44,240
no preemption and circular wait can happen 
simultanously, then deadlocks can occur. 

20
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:49,360
Hopefully, you have found out that 
mutual exclusion is satisfied. 

21
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:55,840
Do we have hold and wait? Yes, if a 
philosopher manages to get the left chopstick,  

22
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:01,120
then the philosopher will not release it until 
he/she manages to get the right chopstick too. 

23
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:06,320
Do we have no preemption? Yes, 2 
chopsticks are released by the same  

24
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:11,520
philosopher that manages to get them once the 
philosopher completes its critical section.

25
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:17,600
Do we have circular waits? 
Indeed, as shown in this slide. 

26
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,920
Hence, this solution is not 
valid, it can lead to a deadlock. 

27
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:25,360
In this slide the arrow from 
the philosopher to the chopstick  

28
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:29,840
means the philosopher is waiting for the 
chopstick. The arrow from the chopstick  

29
00:02:29,840 --> 00:02:34,160
to the philosopher means the 
philosopher got the chopstick.

